#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19fc08ca-72fe-570b-a146-ccf5baf503d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420333} {:answer/id #uuid "2c504700-553a-587d-937f-efdc724865fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420335} {:answer/id #uuid "3d56a853-a4e2-5868-a11b-a8fda7f31398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420336} {:answer/id #uuid "462e1c30-a350-5b46-a5e5-cd8202fb5f1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420337} {:answer/id #uuid "7c57cc06-ff92-5adf-9092-769447c60c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c504700-553a-587d-937f-efdc724865fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420335} :answer-field/id #uuid "3924ef22-234b-5f76-ad06-7ddc5f55eff3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2486be8-75b8-51f1-882e-08f8a79961f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42463" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n\n- $A$ has identical rows\n- $A$ has proportional columns\n- $A$ has a zero row" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have proportional columns.\n\n- $A$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4b47a31-7bda-5366-bac4-1d070776e776" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25029d74-6c31-52ad-8e40-faedc9e473db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420400} {:answer/id #uuid "4d14ebfe-075c-5ad1-ac77-3e3c8fa226f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420401} {:answer/id #uuid "618e807a-28cb-599f-9e9f-ea6004064c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420402} {:answer/id #uuid "b23e5cb8-be50-53e9-bf24-a7374c9dcd80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420403} {:answer/id #uuid "f2516513-8240-538a-acb7-6adbea086ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d14ebfe-075c-5ad1-ac77-3e3c8fa226f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420401} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed0ab4b8-5966-5883-ba7e-805d59df8d8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5414df0-8556-5960-86b0-386e378b451e" :question/math-academy-id "q-213229" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9cddd6584fee52757b1c7a470a6eeffee3678d569e36025ca70c99e5543c71c2.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent a relation, defined on the set $A=\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\delta \\},$ that is neither reflexive nor irreflexive?" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be neither reflective nor irreflexive, some of the points $(\\alpha ,\\alpha ),$ $(\\beta ,\\beta ),$ and $(\\delta ,\\delta )$ must be in $R,$ and others must not.\n\nThese ordered pairs correspond to the diagonal points on the plots, as shown below:\n\n![](images/7d/7d4257c9e249f9a4c2a53c5566292e21ae6dfa7f0e08e27042548da2d8e7e000.png)\n\n \n\nA plot corresponding to a relation that is neither reflexive nor irreflexive must contain some diagonal points, but not all of them. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a84f9d9-81b1-54ae-8ae5-8c05ca06f33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420580} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2e9409-26c5-5b50-9445-3a7e0cc1dff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420581} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0ac365-22bb-5a1d-8ee4-097642b3a96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,B,$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420582} {:answer/id #uuid "dd52b8a6-5559-526c-9e66-cb99de5e9630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420583} {:answer/id #uuid "f63bb298-46ab-531c-9644-243a96de9d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f63bb298-46ab-531c-9644-243a96de9d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420584} :answer-field/id #uuid "0012b7e2-7b21-52ce-a8e6-93abfde08a7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a7b8c64-0e50-5904-90c5-c8b0f74761de" :question/math-academy-id "q-181771" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,-{n}^{2}+n+12\\le 0}$\n\nII. $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,-{m}^{2}+m+12\\ge 0}$\n\nIII. $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,-{q}^{2}+q+12\\ge 0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+x+12$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+x+12 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-x-12) & =0 \\\\ -(x-4)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=4,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/57/57d58b579557ba0ea8c6179bd37209c8199eee83e17d79c74828a051aa1382d6.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+x+12\\le 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx\\le -3\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 4\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [4,\\infty ).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+x+12\\ge 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n-3\\le x\\le 4\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=[-3,4].$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{n}^{2}+n+12\\le 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={…,-4,-3}\\cup {4,5,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{m}^{2}+m+12\\ge 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=8,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $-{q}^{2}+q+12\\ge 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152078 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7345a2-810f-5a35-aa01-092e40df06f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045755e2-d591-54ee-8420-3178cdc956ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420588} {:answer/id #uuid "4cbca457-7b68-56e6-9d5d-1f73524010a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420611} {:answer/id #uuid "72cbce88-fa64-5e61-8cc8-f4a2aeb564ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420621} {:answer/id #uuid "844f10e3-5ed7-5640-92ef-91197a14bf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420631} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a983d6-5780-5e7e-be56-356425ae752f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8a983d6-5780-5e7e-be56-356425ae752f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420668} :answer-field/id #uuid "4355a00e-8da5-5b0a-966f-b3794b720dfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00b6d002-6ca8-5b5f-a710-55bc4f02fb94" :question/math-academy-id "q-216443" :question/problem "$\\,$ ${n}^{2}+7n+12=0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\n- $n\\ge -4$\n- $n=-4$\n- $|n|\\le 4$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}+7n+12=0)\n$$\n\nNote that the solutions to the given equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+7n+12 & =0 \\\\ (n+4)(n+3) & =0 \\\\ n & =-4,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n\\ge -4)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}+7n+12=0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=0,$ then $n\\ge -4,$ but ${n}^{2}+7n+12\\ne 0.$\n\n- Condition II is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n=-4)\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}+7n+12=0)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $n=-4,$ then ${n}^{2}+7n+12=0.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|n|\\le 4)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}+7n+12=0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=0,$ then $|n|\\le 4,$ but ${n}^{2}+7n+12\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152077 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bc3ecd-2da1-5951-abba-95da02dee826" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fdd5fd1-d207-54b2-b6ad-11c0a56f64d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420592} {:answer/id #uuid "5a3107ee-9464-55c3-b2c0-aa35b357416e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420613} {:answer/id #uuid "60328983-7337-550c-99ac-442094612dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420614} {:answer/id #uuid "782a27b8-b034-5c8a-9112-6b1dee6f9a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420623} {:answer/id #uuid "ff258869-ea00-57cc-90ca-d8880f812d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a3107ee-9464-55c3-b2c0-aa35b357416e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420613} :answer-field/id #uuid "53166bf7-c886-5b0e-983e-f872f31bc094" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04618c03-3ca0-590d-b082-33d1176f03c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-172300" :question/problem "$\\,$ ${n}^{2}$ is an even number\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\n- $n$ is an odd number\n- $n$ is a positive number\n- $n$ is a multiple of $2$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an even number})\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions. \n\n- Condition I is *not* a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an even number })\\,⇏\\,(n\\text{ is an odd number})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $n=2$ is not an odd number but ${2}^{2}$ is an even number.\n\n- Condition II is *not* a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an even number})\\,⇏\\,(\\text{ is a positive number})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $n=-2$ is not a positive number, but $(-2)^{2}$ is an even number.\n\n- Condition III is a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an even number})\\,⇒\\,(n\\text{ is a multiple of }2)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSince ${n}^{2}$ is even, it must also be true that $n$ is even. Therefore, $n$ is a multiple of $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152078 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7345a2-810f-5a35-aa01-092e40df06f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e65e4f-7780-5d04-b434-5b982a0cc738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420590} {:answer/id #uuid "45df796e-175e-502d-b952-1965fb870644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420606} {:answer/id #uuid "8108a9c5-ed15-5e27-beb5-e7a64c2d63e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420628} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ad22a1-7d29-515b-8d15-5657d1156609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420659} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b29929-e1a6-5202-b99e-176a6e1f7c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09e65e4f-7780-5d04-b434-5b982a0cc738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420590} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fa13c5f-22a6-5392-8f0c-0c5ab3fb743c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10fd92b2-5e05-5850-b7d2-d8140b9fd04f" :question/math-academy-id "q-172315" :question/problem "$\\,$ ${n}^{2}=16$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\n- $n=4$\n- $-4<n<4$\n- $|n|<16$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}=16)\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {n}^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ (n+4)(n-4) & =0 \\\\ n & =-4,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n=4)\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}=16)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $n=4,$ then ${n}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(-4<n<4)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}=16)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=-1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2} & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\ne 16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|n|<16)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}=16)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=-1,$ then $|n|<16,$ but ${n}^{2}=1\\ne 16.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152078 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7345a2-810f-5a35-aa01-092e40df06f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0141819d-fce9-5dfe-8363-7f37fca55fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420587} {:answer/id #uuid "372fbdd1-3e8c-57d3-b2f8-c9c3f7462ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420602} {:answer/id #uuid "c263e106-7fef-58ab-9b12-f17370ec03a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420650} {:answer/id #uuid "c2efefc8-22d0-53d7-a45f-92c16998fbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420651} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f792f1-74bf-5213-8686-058d2f686f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2efefc8-22d0-53d7-a45f-92c16998fbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420651} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f2ced5e-9852-5fb0-9bab-d51b27fc2767" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "28567992-ba7f-5623-ad07-13abe4be416c" :question/math-academy-id "q-172325" :question/problem "$\\,$ $x+1>4$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\n- $|x|>3$\n- $x>3$\n- $x>4$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,(x+1>4)\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & >4 \\\\ x & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|x|>3)\\,⇏\\,(x+1>4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-4,$ then we have $x+1=-3≯4.$\n\n- Condition II is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(x>3)\\,⇒\\,(x+1>4)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $x>3,$ then $x+1>4.$\n\n- Condition III is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(x>4)\\,⇒\\,(x+1>4)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $x>4,$ then  $x+1>5>4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152079 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "655a0356-bbf0-5769-86d7-6674c65d0f87" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6db338-0cb7-51e2-ac01-95935810d0ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420596} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffe5c1b-c4bb-59cc-9a1d-6ddf172d759f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420604} {:answer/id #uuid "4a289173-5b61-589f-ba66-f52456ff017e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420610} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad66aa9-02b1-539d-9ed6-634cc266d1f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420634} {:answer/id #uuid "aa91d31c-1b7c-51c5-bbcc-d92d41570034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ad66aa9-02b1-539d-9ed6-634cc266d1f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420634} :answer-field/id #uuid "380b7b8c-81dc-5fe3-b1fa-945984d0fd5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "390cc44c-87e5-5905-81de-8b4495a342fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-216459" :question/problem "$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $f$ is a differentiable function\n- $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$\n- $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C,$ for any $C\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the solution to the differential equation ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C\\in R$ is the arbitrary constant.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇍\\,(f\\text{ is a differentiable function})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the function $f(x)={e}^{x}$ is a differentiable function but ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne {sec}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇏\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1,$ the statement ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ is true but $f(x)\\ne \\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1.$\n\n- Condition III is both necessary and sufficient: The statement ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ is true *if and only if* $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C,$ for any $C\\in R.$\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇒\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇐\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇔\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152077 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bc3ecd-2da1-5951-abba-95da02dee826" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16016d71-db47-5c29-a6f2-a697be2b8d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420594} {:answer/id #uuid "1c495edd-fe1e-5f10-9062-4276befeff1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420595} {:answer/id #uuid "80a3cd69-f340-5a70-9070-8ac5442c89a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420627} {:answer/id #uuid "a8debe43-b8b0-55ab-a68f-d6d8ea9b6da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420638} {:answer/id #uuid "acb5c849-cab1-5f29-a8bd-103025ea3634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8debe43-b8b0-55ab-a68f-d6d8ea9b6da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420638} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fdc134d-ac74-5fab-ae76-84aa43f0b703" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b376045-26da-55cb-becc-db924f8d2dac" :question/math-academy-id "q-172302" :question/problem "$\\,$ ${n}^{2}$ is an odd number\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\n- $n$ is odd\n- $n$ is a multiple of $3$\n- $n$ is a multiple of $5$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an odd number})\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions. \n\n- Condition I is a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an odd number})\\,⇒\\,(\\text{ }n\\text{ is odd})\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSince ${n}^{2}$ is odd, it must also be true that $n$ is odd.\n\n- Condition II is *not* a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an odd number})\\,⇏\\,(n\\text{ is a multiple of }3)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $n=7$ is not a multiple of $3,$ but ${n}^{2}=49$ is an odd number.\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an odd number})\\,⇏\\,(n\\text{ is a multiple of }5)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $n=9$ is not a multiple of $5,$ but ${n}^{2}=81$ is an odd number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152079 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "655a0356-bbf0-5769-86d7-6674c65d0f87" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06e8bf70-2a60-5941-87e1-f5189ba873f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420589} {:answer/id #uuid "5970156a-402e-53cd-9465-784a6bc663ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420612} {:answer/id #uuid "78c0055c-c885-59cb-a2a0-5eac59047ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420624} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a7bdcd-3396-5c6e-a601-a161e66d71fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420652} {:answer/id #uuid "f78301a0-d9e6-54f9-81a3-411506bab620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f78301a0-d9e6-54f9-81a3-411506bab620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420666} :answer-field/id #uuid "be0157e5-7ad5-51d5-b39b-5c2649601c3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5904ff03-6479-54c0-a38b-31e73684000e" :question/math-academy-id "q-216449" :question/problem "$\\,$ $12∣x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $2∣x$ and $6∣x$\n- $3∣x$ and $4∣x$\n- $3∣x$ and $8∣x$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇍\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }6∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=6$ is divisible by $2$ and by $6$ but not divisible by $12.$\n\n- Condition II is both necessary and sufficient: A number is divisible by $12$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $3$ and $4.$\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇒\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }4∣x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇐\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }4∣x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇔\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }4∣x)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇏\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }8∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=12$ is divisible by $12$ but not divisible by $8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152077 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bc3ecd-2da1-5951-abba-95da02dee826" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e7d224-a6a4-59f8-8fdd-0ff468f330c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420593} {:answer/id #uuid "266cebd5-1df8-59d8-9d7f-5e97061db023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420597} {:answer/id #uuid "49f2aae6-e3f9-535f-82d1-b68b36c62b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420609} {:answer/id #uuid "ae91a6ae-e621-5467-9306-e9dd3d875c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420644} {:answer/id #uuid "f3cd468a-9cc8-5dc8-8fd8-8420399559dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "266cebd5-1df8-59d8-9d7f-5e97061db023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420597} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dddff4c-3d97-5829-a5f6-3f51ccd09496" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ef2cacc-c405-55b2-8430-66b58129ad71" :question/math-academy-id "q-172298" :question/problem "$\\,$ $|x-2|=6$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\n- $x>4$\n- $|x|<10$\n- $x=8$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-2| & =6 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 6 \\\\ x & =-4,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇏\\,(x>4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-4,$ then $|x-2|=6,$ but $x≯4.$\n\n- Condition II is a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇒\\,(|x|<10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nEach solution of $|x-2|=6$ satisfies $|x|<10.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇏\\,(x=8)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-4,$ then $|x-2|=6,$ but $x\\ne 8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152079 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "655a0356-bbf0-5769-86d7-6674c65d0f87" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ce83eb5-512d-54ec-a688-0337fd49e4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420603} {:answer/id #uuid "41c86782-467c-5503-8573-af98661637c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420605} {:answer/id #uuid "686fb17a-2611-5f1c-bdba-c5c19a1d121a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420619} {:answer/id #uuid "83a71146-83dd-5de6-95b8-b27059ff5408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420630} {:answer/id #uuid "bba394c5-1fc5-5d3a-b4f9-867f1cffef57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41c86782-467c-5503-8573-af98661637c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420605} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d8a18c8-c94d-53a1-9598-3f56ab83bdc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e1662ac-5299-5cc7-92ca-68567f121fc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-183429" :question/problem "$\\,$ $20∣x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $2∣x$ and $10∣x$\n- $4∣x$ and $5∣x$\n- $100∣x$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇍\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }10∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=10$ is divisible by $2$ and by $10$ but not divisible by $20.$\n\n- Condition II is both necessary and sufficient: A number is divisible by $20$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $4$ and $5.$\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇒\\,(4∣x\\text{ and }5∣x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇐\\,(4∣x\\text{ and }5∣x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇔\\,(4∣x\\text{ and }5∣x)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇏\\,(100∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=20$ is divisible by $20$ but not divisible by $100.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152079 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "655a0356-bbf0-5769-86d7-6674c65d0f87" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a5084f6-69e4-520c-a5e4-b275a8c4693a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420598} {:answer/id #uuid "48cc24d8-8ada-5963-8ea2-3d096ef3b9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420608} {:answer/id #uuid "67b27e1c-7265-5812-b062-c84b86be6606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420618} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc9b04f-28fc-5724-95bd-6fe1adf8f451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420633} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e878cb-1ec8-5097-aa5d-f6e3f76faa62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bc9b04f-28fc-5724-95bd-6fe1adf8f451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420633} :answer-field/id #uuid "dae23ffb-af9a-509a-99d1-3643141889bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9dd0c0c2-7652-5690-ae78-53d067f574fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-183485" :question/problem "$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=2$\n- $f(x)={x}^{2}+C,$ for any $C\\in R$\n- $f(x)={x}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the solution to the differential equation ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int 2x\\,dx \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C\\in R$ is the arbitrary constant.\n\nMoreover, the differential equation ${f}^{″}(x)=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\int 2\\,dx \\\\  & =2x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D\\in R$ is another arbitrary constant.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇍\\,({f}^{″}(x)=2)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+x,$ we have ${f}^{′}(x)=2x+1,$ and so the statement ${f}^{″}(x)=2$  is true but ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 2x.$\n\n- Condition II is both necessary and sufficient: The statement ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ is true *if and only if* $f(x)={x}^{2}+C,$ for any $C\\in R.$\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇒\\,(f(x)={x}^{2}+C)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇐\\,(f(x)={x}^{2}+C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇔\\,(f(x)={x}^{2}+C)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇏\\,(f(x)={x}^{2})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+1$ the statement ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ is true but $f(x)\\ne {x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152078 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7345a2-810f-5a35-aa01-092e40df06f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2f79458-4c2b-54d4-87ba-d97c53f760aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420635} {:answer/id #uuid "a41ae0db-cfac-58d5-a3cd-32a56a56b7c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420636} {:answer/id #uuid "b877dce5-c49c-5f58-9963-36f0cb4433cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420647} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ec35dc-2bc0-5873-a893-a0c100b00486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420657} {:answer/id #uuid "fec036cb-7688-5ab9-89fc-cd9daabe9e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2f79458-4c2b-54d4-87ba-d97c53f760aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420635} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecfcee5e-24eb-56dc-ac84-c2b7a4109c97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4cdd56d-10e6-5abf-be98-585e1ce87428" :question/math-academy-id "q-172322" :question/problem "$\\,$ $a\\ge 10$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\n- $a=10$\n- $a>15$\n- $a>5$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a=10)\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $a=10,$ then $a\\ge 10.$\n\n- Condition II is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a>15)\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $a>15,$ then $a\\ge 10.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a>5)\\,⇏\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $a=7,$ then we have $a≱10.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152077 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bc3ecd-2da1-5951-abba-95da02dee826" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "723b0b18-8d2a-573d-8c7a-b060274c4be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420620} {:answer/id #uuid "79d4f89d-2406-5661-97b9-53a4d47d9095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420625} {:answer/id #uuid "c63ae798-9fea-58a3-a28e-246e0b2e732f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420653} {:answer/id #uuid "f2226bbc-d85c-51dc-b76d-73e6d3a402ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420660} {:answer/id #uuid "f4f07888-45ef-54ff-800d-2f443444b95a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "723b0b18-8d2a-573d-8c7a-b060274c4be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420620} :answer-field/id #uuid "65d6a9dc-fcc4-5ded-8f64-2cbe1ae88d98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ed1378b7-d665-5e7d-b9bb-42ac28cb1db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-216436" :question/problem "$\\,$ ${x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\n- $x({x}^{2}+x-6)\\le 0$\n- $x\\le 2$\n- $x\\ge 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given inequality is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-6 & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & \\le 0 \\\\ -3\\le x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇏\\,(x({x}^{2}+x-6)\\le 0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-1,$ then ${x}^{2}+x-6=-6\\le 0,$ but $x({x}^{2}+x-6)=6≰0.$\n\n- Condition II is a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇒\\,(x\\le 2)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nEvery solution of ${x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0$ satisfies $x\\le 2.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇏\\,(x\\ge 0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-1,$ then ${x}^{2}+x-6=-6\\le 0,$ but $x≱0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152079 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "655a0356-bbf0-5769-86d7-6674c65d0f87" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca9ed22-18ac-5eeb-9f53-e13bcad40e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420591} {:answer/id #uuid "2b1c1196-eb9d-51a7-8da8-862fff9a154f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420599} {:answer/id #uuid "7ffe0ac0-8a5a-58ee-a4f9-48dcb58891b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420626} {:answer/id #uuid "81e54969-5398-5218-be00-7fbc3b23054c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420629} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6d3a5f-e04d-5107-9d35-a0710f0ab915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf6d3a5f-e04d-5107-9d35-a0710f0ab915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420654} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdfba46-cdcd-52d6-a6f1-f3d15b8a74a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f653d451-6beb-59b1-affd-6d539e9553ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-216447" :question/problem "$\\,$ $18∣x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $2∣x$ and $9∣x$\n- $3∣x$ and $6∣x$\n- $72∣x$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is both necessary and sufficient: A number is divisible by $18$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $2$ and $9.$\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇒\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇐\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇔\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇍\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }6∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=6$ is divisible by $3$ and by $6$ but not divisible by $18.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇏\\,(72∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=18$ is divisible by $18$ but not divisible by $72.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d52570-2557-595f-99e2-1b31c2d60dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420857} {:answer/id #uuid "4525744b-a33c-5d02-b83b-0cd4fd30e353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420866} {:answer/id #uuid "8da70b03-f65d-5594-a91e-19d3b3531590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420880} {:answer/id #uuid "c78e6bb5-9afc-5b31-b499-d02078202d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420889} {:answer/id #uuid "e1eda114-26bd-5178-9bcc-2a69974022c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c78e6bb5-9afc-5b31-b499-d02078202d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420889} :answer-field/id #uuid "6033533a-9e89-5e03-a1f8-d82826d8190e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ae03bac-e2a8-5c3b-a5da-8c8f23590a03" :question/math-academy-id "q-127026" :question/problem "![](images/28/28a7eb32cfcd800c907e9f4af927eca7bd8616d36162682413db62a9feabefbb.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/fe/fecf59674259d41222ffd981b1ab6d90c474fe2b0e37a6d5f7626f34536a6896.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/56/56a7fc09ed2a2a15b0949fa6041dc9c0ee6beb69f30b1a5c048afd8c57887d52.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05372253-4ce0-51d2-8c8a-6be80ef5e71b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420856} {:answer/id #uuid "3efe06e4-d0d0-5a68-8a81-622c991444de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420865} {:answer/id #uuid "5c350aee-ec57-55e6-825e-9ebcb6d46c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420870} {:answer/id #uuid "9073e798-9345-5c89-aa11-6f536f9781c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420882} {:answer/id #uuid "dd620493-1352-5d6a-ab86-993b4208f2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05372253-4ce0-51d2-8c8a-6be80ef5e71b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420856} :answer-field/id #uuid "99e2f9e7-a7ef-5d8d-abe6-b747079ed849" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cc7cc51-6281-53f8-b74f-55c2a0b2fa6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127024" :question/problem "![](images/01/01d8d85064253b9241b1fd93c22381897f23556d6e02c5c2c0161c94cee3bbdf.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *in* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb4fd4068760bb9132052b26508f0e1e0c02c45d425770de804b17ce7c512919.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is negative, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/46/46afd4cde01c95388c9daefbef0668ef696d5817ab9ea3614982edf58a9bdf55.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29facd25-cb5e-5912-bb18-0cf1f7a74908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420862} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5a907e-06a3-50b5-82c0-14c80eee68f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420864} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb97c0d-6343-53ce-a7a9-c6e8df480d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420871} {:answer/id #uuid "b1686369-3dee-56da-b3d5-fd5ec9dbeb68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420884} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e131d5-1b27-5110-aeb8-3ce97e516ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29facd25-cb5e-5912-bb18-0cf1f7a74908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420862} :answer-field/id #uuid "9864b2a4-0034-5f3d-ba1e-cf4c021c366a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e18756f-1e50-5f09-be71-26f001c4f68e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127027" :question/problem "![](images/af/afaec113101f1a63134d2b1cb5c9744b93f9b84dabafaa205780d2dc5b49a7bc.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/6b/6b24ece279cc480f20d7a4dbc65f7a5257af71f22688b1416c73e3b40673a321.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/9e/9e8527d26f0f2bd5372d8643f8f9321ec022bb0079e1fc5b23be4978f436613c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1675d456-4b28-58a6-bb2c-fed556137a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420861} {:answer/id #uuid "30474622-ff1f-54c7-ae65-580701a7082c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420863} {:answer/id #uuid "4902a853-9300-5dc4-9829-f0878517ea35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420868} {:answer/id #uuid "90a3ed78-ff8d-5b31-8fe9-66647638b119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420883} {:answer/id #uuid "c14caedd-1925-5e81-b3fb-2d1a39f46989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90a3ed78-ff8d-5b31-8fe9-66647638b119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420883} :answer-field/id #uuid "406f40cf-3f8f-59a9-9607-1816ee0ec44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "765f030b-c4cb-527c-8c26-c947b0ace6a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127028" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f1d25942ae680c068b587f38bfbc968c8850b8fa2db2003e994e1ec32dad892.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Note that the flux $F$ that flows in through $C$ is the same that flows out through $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/b7/b7acc3028de66ae71ae4e425da639e51a357a77f2b0a345290592cac91ba17d3.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/04/04639a0074aa4607fa57bfe589aad1780f8da6f573d8589a68c2fdb569b5a129.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fca5176-1359-5ad8-b012-592ffa9b20b5" :knowledge-point/title "Cartesian Products With Three Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "323a0eaa-c97b-52de-a3bc-ebc72ae2e5d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420963} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1c11f8-7017-508a-a025-032d058eefc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420965} {:answer/id #uuid "9c53a0ac-9594-5d9f-871e-69dd75c6ab8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420973} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ed4afd-f54e-59d9-bd0a-604a3f0d6358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420977} {:answer/id #uuid "df07081f-dcec-5eb4-b4c9-6e0e88404217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c53a0ac-9594-5d9f-871e-69dd75c6ab8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420973} :answer-field/id #uuid "f342874d-f251-593c-aa5a-839fe9ae8390" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c44203d-f0c3-504e-a004-3addf0955223" :question/math-academy-id "q-181956" :question/problem "If $A=\\{-2,2\\}$ and $B=\\{c,d\\},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A\\times B)\\times B?$\n\nI. $(-2,d,c)$\n\nII. $(-2,(c,d))$\n\nIII. $((2,d),c)$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${-2,2}$ with ${c,d},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/98/98e7cfca515290b952ea5aefc9b2516a5cd6a68de0dc048c2075128e6c60b651.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(-2,c),(-2,d),(2,c),(2,d)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A\\times B)\\times B\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A\\times B$ and $B,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/1e/1e96aeb1c5bfb1f9faa590a8eb8b01528a1ce9431a3f1ceac92b156a9bdde221.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A\\times B)\\times B|=|A\\times B|\\cdot |B|=4\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $((2,d),c)\\in (A\\times B)\\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c26aeaa-9252-5bd3-9ad6-0fc25ca6b03c" :knowledge-point/title "Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef0d8fa-6fbc-553b-b26f-6d47fc529632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421008} {:answer/id #uuid "a92c8a27-c90f-55ba-900b-012f540a5175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421071} {:answer/id #uuid "ac43712d-08c1-5314-bc53-7add8e989850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421075} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a82ac1-260b-5de1-9737-cad96665d7a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421082} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5963c4-6280-5a68-adcd-4bcc40dceab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb5963c4-6280-5a68-adcd-4bcc40dceab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421100} :answer-field/id #uuid "f88db5da-74c7-5a6c-8f64-068574198241" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03f10976-674d-5f7f-9c6a-ec5de8c4e5cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-183510" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x=-1$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ $(x-1)(x+1)=0$\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n\n- $x=-1$ is sufficient for $(x-1)(x+1)=0$\n- $x=-1$ is necessary for $(x-1)(x+1)=0$\n- For $(x-1)(x+1)=0,$ it is necessary that $x=-1$\n- For $x=-1,$ it is sufficient that $(x-1)(x+1)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe have the following grammatical constructions for $S⇒N:$\n\n- $S$ is sufficient for $N$\n\n- For $N,$ it is sufficient that $S$\n\n- $N$ is necessary for $S$\n\n- For $S,$ it is necessary that $N$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n{{x=-1}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is sufficient for}\\,{{(x-1)(x+1)=0}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n{{x=-1}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is necessary for}\\,{{(x-1)(x+1)=0}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{(x-1)(x+1)=0,}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{it is necessary that}\\,{{x=-1}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option IV reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{x=-1,}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{it is sufficient that}\\,{{(x-1)(x+1)=0}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198282 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0809c075-e1be-5d49-b1fe-5433114bcf16" :knowledge-point/title "\"If-Then\", \"If\", \"Only If\", and \"Implies\""}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093f27fc-7e6f-5b74-9e0f-56f1dcd8da8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421004} {:answer/id #uuid "27753478-b4dc-5f6b-9f5d-e4c8cf0b2741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421016} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf28c27-5628-51c2-afd9-674c7904768f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421037} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1f026b-b1a3-571d-937c-f08cf77e8b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421042} {:answer/id #uuid "a241a06c-b9b1-53dc-aa4f-bc3f27232754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e1f026b-b1a3-571d-937c-f08cf77e8b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421042} :answer-field/id #uuid "7423f83a-bd3f-57b5-a6e1-9ee4c5646676" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0e0b252f-632c-5fb4-955e-f2c2686886d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-183448" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x=2$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ ${x}^{2}=4$\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n\n- ${x}^{2}=4$ implies $x=2$\n- If ${x}^{2}=4,$ then $x=2$\n- ${x}^{2}=4$ only if $x=2$\n- ${x}^{2}=4$ if $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the following grammatical constructions for the implication $P⇒Q:$\n\n- If $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $P$ only if $Q$\n\n- $Q$ if $P$\n\n- $P$ implies $Q$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n{{{x}^{2}=4}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{implies}\\,{{x=2}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n\\text{if}\\,{{{x}^{2}=4}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)},\\,\\text{then}\\,{{x=2}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n{{{x}^{2}=4}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{only if}\\,{{x=2}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option IV reads\n\n$$\n{{{x}^{2}=4}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{if}\\,{{x=2}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b24ae815-5706-5b65-bab8-2d67f8405c9c" :knowledge-point/title "\"Whenever\", and \"Provided That\""}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d40fcad-2b54-5f09-b54d-9a821f10c93a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421006} {:answer/id #uuid "29ff0373-60cd-5181-92d4-2b66865dbf72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421018} {:answer/id #uuid "988b9e54-2317-550f-8181-5d56c55e2bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421059} {:answer/id #uuid "e436142a-6fc6-52cb-bacc-09d95d051cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421095} {:answer/id #uuid "fe58e7c2-f1a1-57fe-86bd-e109566044a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d40fcad-2b54-5f09-b54d-9a821f10c93a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421006} :answer-field/id #uuid "88cc20d1-1323-5c7b-9afb-5b2500b0cb08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50849cc0-0b42-5b52-abb5-e0bdf56880ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-183568" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $0\\le x<1$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ $|x|<1$\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n\n- Whenever $|x|<1,$ then $0\\le x<1$\n- $0\\le x<1$ whenever $|x|<1$\n- $|x|<1,$ provided that $0\\le x<1$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $P⇒Q$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- Whenever $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $Q$ whenever $P$\n\n- $Q,$ provided that $P$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n\\text{Whenever}\\,{{|x|<1}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)},\\,\\text{then}\\,{{0\\le x<1}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n{{0\\le x<1}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{whenever}\\,{{|x|<1}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n{{|x|<1\\text{,}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{provided that}\\,{{0\\le x<1}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b24ae815-5706-5b65-bab8-2d67f8405c9c" :knowledge-point/title "\"Whenever\", and \"Provided That\""}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320b80df-22ed-5e98-ad73-2f1d0474fa78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421024} {:answer/id #uuid "41f5ede5-5a8e-58dc-84d4-9753affb83f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421034} {:answer/id #uuid "59e660dd-80d7-5d3d-9256-060d4013846a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421041} {:answer/id #uuid "9df674b4-222e-5226-8472-65f74b544b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421064} {:answer/id #uuid "addc3e96-7972-50b2-ae08-63e88d8f6e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "320b80df-22ed-5e98-ad73-2f1d0474fa78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421024} :answer-field/id #uuid "b060c187-cb85-5841-a5af-47c6d7c784ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78f9226d-96f1-55af-96d5-28d4a76505e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-183533" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $4\\,|\\,x$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ $x$ is composite\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n\n- Whenever $4\\,|\\,x,$ then $x$ is composite\n- $4\\,|\\,x$ whenever $x$ is composite\n- $x$ is composite, provided that $4\\,|\\,x$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $P⇒Q$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- Whenever $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $Q$ whenever $P$\n\n- $Q,$ provided that $P$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n\\text{Whenever}\\,{{4\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)},\\,\\text{then}\\,{{x\\text{ is composite}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n{{4\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{whenever}\\,{{x\\text{ is composite}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is composite,}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{provided that}\\,{{4\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b24ae815-5706-5b65-bab8-2d67f8405c9c" :knowledge-point/title "\"Whenever\", and \"Provided That\""}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a8ce566-6890-582f-a158-b42ec0895248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421005} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1c3f5e-ef37-5163-947d-6521fd9e875a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421045} {:answer/id #uuid "9bdb2a41-ea7b-512e-8918-cc67d1d238e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421061} {:answer/id #uuid "9d633d9a-da33-5e88-94db-67f637b7f7e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421063} {:answer/id #uuid "fde546f5-de83-5d7c-8c8e-c9d84f7e0b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d633d9a-da33-5e88-94db-67f637b7f7e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421063} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb5ffcf8-bc22-542f-9944-294204fb67c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7bd7ef2d-fe07-5622-9cee-22e524115939" :question/math-academy-id "q-183566" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x=0$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ ${x}^{2}=x$\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n\n- Whenever $x=0,$ then ${x}^{2}=x$\n- ${x}^{2}=x$ whenever $x=0$\n- $x=0,$ provided that ${x}^{2}=x$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $P⇒Q$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- Whenever $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $Q$ whenever $P$\n\n- $Q,$ provided that $P$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n\\text{Whenever}\\,{{x=0\\text{,}}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{then}\\,{{{x}^{2}=x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n{{{x}^{2}=x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{whenever}\\,{{x=0}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n{{x=0\\text{,}}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{provided that}\\,{{{x}^{2}=x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40772f11-35fc-5f4f-bda3-7d228823b749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421258} {:answer/id #uuid "4526afa8-bdbb-5355-a82f-899c0edd5093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421261} {:answer/id #uuid "8096790f-b2a3-5ac1-9707-fe01377b65c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421272} {:answer/id #uuid "80faef7f-76e5-5c4b-8486-df12f7c6f160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421273} {:answer/id #uuid "c81d1e22-0260-5ad3-9abd-8fe77f612e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80faef7f-76e5-5c4b-8486-df12f7c6f160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421273} :answer-field/id #uuid "f206bfdf-3b37-567e-9742-b62602b37fa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46d6b627-57fc-5c4f-9c8a-dc31a7241e7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334252" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =1 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(0)-x(1)+x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2)-x(2x)+{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f9502e-19c3-58bb-98da-1562e0b0dfd0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69c8f651-7fad-58ab-9225-eb16c24dc65f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421344} {:answer/id #uuid "90760cd8-7871-5634-b9b5-3531a983c1aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421354} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b3f0ee-7ced-5feb-9405-387aa70e3545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421361} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5dc06e-ed8a-53d3-bee4-a48767f1eff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421378} {:answer/id #uuid "fec83f8f-fd76-54ba-94a1-1cd6f83935d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc5dc06e-ed8a-53d3-bee4-a48767f1eff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421378} :answer-field/id #uuid "763d9cb9-42d8-5b2a-baa3-fc94d026c898" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c07a7ee-9fb6-5236-b864-38ec61e9b0cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-214241" :question/problem "Which of the following are equivalence relations on $P(N)?$\n\n- $A⊄B$\n- $|A|\\ne |B|$\n- $A⊇B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- The relation \"$⊄$\" is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since ${2,3}⊄{2}$ but ${2}⊂{2,3}.$\n\n- The relation \"$\\ne $\" for the cardinalities of sets is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive since $|{2}|=|{2}|.$\n\n- The relation \"$⊇$\" is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since $N⊇{1}$ but ${1}⊉N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f9502e-19c3-58bb-98da-1562e0b0dfd0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "589ce36f-fb09-55ca-ab3b-7f3aa6166186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421337} {:answer/id #uuid "661dd547-fab0-5221-9f01-e96464c7b3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421343} {:answer/id #uuid "a186ab13-4673-549f-8754-6fcd73c7e651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421360} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8cf821-8c18-5dd6-be5b-f58b8b837325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421374} {:answer/id #uuid "d79a90aa-2e9b-5f04-b081-64d7cc3f75e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a186ab13-4673-549f-8754-6fcd73c7e651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421360} :answer-field/id #uuid "da731a85-b255-5017-87f3-60d7f37421d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1a65003f-5b78-57dd-8ad6-282231fb787b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76164" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $R?$\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if $xy>0$\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if $x=2y$\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if $x-y$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because $0\\cdot 0=0≯0.$\n\n- Relation II is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric because $2=2(1)$ but $1\\ne 2(2).$\n\n- Relation III is an equivalence relation:\n\n- It is reflexive because for all $x\\in R,$ we have $x-x=0$ is a rational number.\n\n- It is symmetric because for all $x,y\\in R,$ if $x-y=r$ is a rational number, then $y-x=-(x-y)=-r$ is also a rational number.\n\n- It is transitive because for all $x,y,z\\in R,$ if $x-y=r$ and $y-z=q,$ where $r,q$ are rational numbers, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-z=(x-y)+(y-z)=r+q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis also a rational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f9502e-19c3-58bb-98da-1562e0b0dfd0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040a02e2-1bb9-514c-903f-81f6a2d7ea14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421315} {:answer/id #uuid "17261494-8ee1-5a6c-a36e-65e458355a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421318} {:answer/id #uuid "18e496f1-8b52-5668-a3c0-762eb1ea9fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421319} {:answer/id #uuid "62992426-d8cb-559d-b581-acc3f07854b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421341} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c7b953-d54f-55fc-9672-1c5535f4bd2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18e496f1-8b52-5668-a3c0-762eb1ea9fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421319} :answer-field/id #uuid "f758d976-bcdb-5e5f-af93-d6835069d66d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c8d639d-4ba9-5766-b837-cc9cfbce29a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-214245" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $P(N)?$\n\n- $A\\,R\\,B$ if $A\\cap B=∅$\n- $A⊃B$\n- $A=B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${1}\\cap {1}\\ne ∅.$\n\n- Relation II is also not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${1}⊅{1}.$\n\n- Relation III is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "968df6ec-e87e-5770-9c11-e7c8d8a27e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations on Finite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2945124f-634b-58b9-9263-8fb5d84a8223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421322} {:answer/id #uuid "529bac0f-0ab2-5dd2-829d-897c7a169213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421333} {:answer/id #uuid "71db4ad8-a7c1-5de6-a688-cac7a9af590c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421349} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d02066-46a0-505f-9c99-5c4bf970eed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421363} {:answer/id #uuid "e584f397-eac7-55f4-9aac-875dd305d2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2945124f-634b-58b9-9263-8fb5d84a8223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421322} :answer-field/id #uuid "e39d6c66-19f1-5b1f-bd77-f03b05414e51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72d1402f-e4b5-5dff-b1dc-9142e68fa197" :question/math-academy-id "q-214274" :question/problem "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A={\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }$ defined as \n\n$$\nR={(\\alpha ,\\alpha ),(\\beta ,\\beta ),(\\beta ,\\alpha ),(\\alpha ,\\beta )}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $R$ is reflexive\n- $R$ is symmetric\n- $R$ is transitive\n- $R$ is an equivalence relation" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is *not* reflexive because $(\\gamma ,\\gamma )\\notin R.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric because $(y,x)\\in R$ whenever $(x,y)\\in R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive because for all $x,y,z\\in {\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma },$ if $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,z)\\in R,$ then $(x,z)\\in R.$ Recall that it's sufficient to check only the cases when both pairs have different components: \n\n- $(\\alpha ,\\beta )\\in R$ and $(\\beta ,\\alpha )\\in R,$ and also $(\\alpha ,\\alpha )\\in R$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $(\\beta ,\\alpha )\\in R$ and $(\\alpha ,\\beta )\\in R,$ and also $(\\beta ,\\beta )\\in R$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation is *not* an equivalence relation since it's not reflexive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b70dd396-b01a-51e6-a52c-8ff729f48c45" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Standard Relation Is an Equivalence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b40b244-4bab-58ee-bc64-fa1d33519579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421317} {:answer/id #uuid "52627dc2-eb63-52a8-8d5f-f961978b8947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421332} {:answer/id #uuid "5c380eff-8271-5e3c-a697-38aee4ec4cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421338} {:answer/id #uuid "7560d387-5026-5568-9eb3-53e8af6d9cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421350} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a23ef4-cc98-56f0-ac39-ae3829545ef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52627dc2-eb63-52a8-8d5f-f961978b8947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421332} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eb12818-1e53-50fb-9686-e8410139c5cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72fa77bc-6d37-516a-bc08-8cef3db1618e" :question/math-academy-id "q-214252" :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $N.$\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,y∤x\n$$\n\n Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $R$ is reflexive\n- $R$ is symmetric\n- $R$ is transitive\n- $R$ is an equivalence relation" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is not reflexive. For example, $3∣3.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is not symmetric. For example, $9∤3$ but $3∣9.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is not transitive. For example, $3∤2$ and $2∤9,$ but $3∣9.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is not an equivalence relation since it's neither reflexive nor symmetric nor transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f9502e-19c3-58bb-98da-1562e0b0dfd0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4af48504-6f1c-5b90-87ab-46f546fb2eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421329} {:answer/id #uuid "6d984c53-eae2-5d52-bffe-bead8d751ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421346} {:answer/id #uuid "810ae78b-09ba-5372-b677-ed900baea3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421351} {:answer/id #uuid "8a83063d-29fe-5569-893d-885aaf2bcb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421353} {:answer/id #uuid "d179e1fc-abb6-5aca-92d5-246063f7fbc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4af48504-6f1c-5b90-87ab-46f546fb2eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421329} :answer-field/id #uuid "376584a2-7252-545c-be81-7372124fc83e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78aa39d2-6d95-5a7b-bd86-1b4658d3d903" :question/math-academy-id "q-214247" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $Q?$\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4$\n- $x=y$\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if $xy\\in N$" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${2}^{2}+{2}^{2}≰4.$\n\n- Relation II is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\n- Relation III is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\notin N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f9502e-19c3-58bb-98da-1562e0b0dfd0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2224d9bb-0faf-5fc9-b610-28aaddc41507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421321} {:answer/id #uuid "914a9fe7-3947-5a9f-92ed-734d047aebca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421355} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e40a81-ea5a-5e10-8335-f943e082f51c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421366} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6d8781-5676-564f-b9b9-dac7cffccc49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421371} {:answer/id #uuid "ce69759e-4547-5b71-b4e3-eb3dc5f1b96e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7e40a81-ea5a-5e10-8335-f943e082f51c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421366} :answer-field/id #uuid "36c1e14d-f406-53c3-919e-0c1fd156cc01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3f6ced1-e3e7-51b2-bdb3-c0561413aee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-214246" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $N?$\n\n- $a\\,R\\,b$ if $a-b$ is a multiple of $2$\n- $a∣b$\n- $a\\,R\\,b$ if $3∣(a+b)$" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is an equivalence relation. \n\n- It is reflexive because for all $a\\in N,$ we have that $a-a=0$ is a multiple of $2.$\n\n- It is symmetric because for all $a,b\\in N,$ if $a-b$ is a multiple of $2,$ then $b-a$ is also a multiple of $2.$\n\n- It is transitive because for all $a,b,c\\in N,$ if $a-b$ is a multiple of $2$ and $b-c$ is a multiple of $2,$ then\n\n$$\na-c=(a-b)+(b-c)\n$$\n\nis also a multiple of $2.$\n\n- Relation II is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since $3∣6$ but $6∤3.$\n\n- Relation III is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive since $3∤(1+1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fca5176-1359-5ad8-b012-592ffa9b20b5" :knowledge-point/title "Cartesian Products With Three Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bfc8b18-19ed-5d03-a98a-c6f208ab5bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421472} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3a5443-4b0e-550e-bf7e-6d0557498925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421474} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1e2c2a-e3a1-5fd0-a837-50714e2a444d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421477} {:answer/id #uuid "e723cf0c-c777-5b0e-8e18-bb6ad7a41a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421479} {:answer/id #uuid "f574563b-f694-5f1d-9ca7-08a37b989e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e723cf0c-c777-5b0e-8e18-bb6ad7a41a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421479} :answer-field/id #uuid "22e20c2f-0ad2-5c27-92ce-f6cb31d11c7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd7d0702-ab54-5763-af3c-93c657eacc9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-181974" :question/problem "If $A=\\{1,2,3\\}$ and $B=\\{x,y\\},$ which of the following elements belong to $(B\\times B)\\times A?$\n\nI. $(x,y,1)$\n\nII. $((x,x),1)$\n\nIII. $((y,x),2)$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $B\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${x,y}$ with ${x,y},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/5a/5af389ab817ce509519af7a3c1a5fdbb583d8e13859b9aa3b6166958f81e3040.png)\n\nHence, the set $B\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nB\\times B={(x,x),(x,y),(y,x),(y,y)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|B\\times B|=|B|\\cdot |B|=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(B\\times B)\\times A\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $B\\times B$ and $A,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/da/dae7febad357be8d9f000c9dc7abfbe97fda34e3e9ff0b0e8ab0289e3c4e782a.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(B\\times B)\\times A|=|B\\times B|\\cdot |A|=4\\cdot 3=12.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $((x,x),1)$ and $((y,x),2)$ are in $(B\\times B)\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3601ac83-0800-567a-9a0d-4d5c37cef413" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to an Equivalence Class"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c166d58-96a2-5983-ae04-48fd94ad9bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421643} {:answer/id #uuid "543e9849-f4a4-5bbb-8761-4948634144e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421656} {:answer/id #uuid "cac53ec3-ca9a-580f-8859-c15b54c1a6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421689} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f10726-4c28-598f-8721-377b23cb1f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421691} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d0b779-1c8e-53da-9eb6-c9fdd1455bf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6d0b779-1c8e-53da-9eb6-c9fdd1455bf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421692} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe48b365-00e3-52fe-9ee6-28adef726d37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "071418af-96d7-52c9-8845-42c31b88cc12" :question/math-academy-id "q-77200" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{3}={y}^{3}.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[8]?$\n\nI. $-2$\n\nII. $2$\n\nIII. $8$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[8]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $8$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8] & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼8} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,{x}^{3}={8}^{3}} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x=8} \\\\  & ={8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $8$ is in $[8],$ while $-2$ and $2$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3601ac83-0800-567a-9a0d-4d5c37cef413" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to an Equivalence Class"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bd8b0ab-150b-54d0-81ad-62745455f13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421653} {:answer/id #uuid "5e428509-d761-5d25-9064-b0a933d1e087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421660} {:answer/id #uuid "65034044-ad36-5de4-9218-9c6031ac2efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421663} {:answer/id #uuid "a11d855a-0bb1-51c3-a321-8b28ec6504e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421677} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f156b1-f4c7-51f2-8b59-ff35f351faee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65034044-ad36-5de4-9218-9c6031ac2efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421663} :answer-field/id #uuid "9412c8de-1e86-51d7-8119-c14b13217534" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "22db406c-85ef-5a5a-ab22-d062645b4362" :question/math-academy-id "q-221718" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $7\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[5]?$\n\nI. $12$\n\nII. $-4$\n\nIII. $-2$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[5]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $5$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[5] & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x∼5} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,7\\,|\\,(x-5)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x=5+7k,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-9,-2,5,12,19,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $12$ and $-2$ are in $[5],$ while $-4$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3601ac83-0800-567a-9a0d-4d5c37cef413" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to an Equivalence Class"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15e14667-0aa0-583f-8209-0c7938abde55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421639} {:answer/id #uuid "b90a23b8-30d5-5c66-9bec-51828ed054a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421679} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf68f06-aef2-50e7-b2b3-5af7e8f1bb85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421680} {:answer/id #uuid "c38d0ace-0856-55bb-81d0-6cb2539ca988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421686} {:answer/id #uuid "ef394b6b-c6c2-50f9-812e-b8ed02458a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c38d0ace-0856-55bb-81d0-6cb2539ca988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421686} :answer-field/id #uuid "427832c3-ff8d-5621-b856-408a0605c9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c1db003-08b1-562e-8980-51389652bb7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-77205" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $C,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{2}={y}^{2}.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[-4]?$\n\nI. $-4$\n\nII. $4$\n\nIII. $4\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[-4]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $-4$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-4] & ={x\\in C\\,|\\,x∼-4} \\\\  & ={x\\in C\\,|\\,{x}^{2}=(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in C\\,|\\,{x}^{2}=16} \\\\  & ={-4,4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-4$ and $4$ are in $[-4],$ while $4\\text{i}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dacfbca6-6135-5d51-a07e-627fce79dfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "209a9659-21f3-5335-86ea-8fb565a19aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421640} {:answer/id #uuid "82a555b5-7e6b-57ec-a220-0322b30bb853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421669} {:answer/id #uuid "c26909a8-bef0-5d9a-ab7f-4e353af1d2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421685} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b0d093-23ef-5fe1-b0e2-1ccf2620a605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421694} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d0756d-3751-51c4-a0cc-0133cbd88b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82a555b5-7e6b-57ec-a220-0322b30bb853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421669} :answer-field/id #uuid "88e9daad-aa9b-5549-bee8-d7bdbb79efb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46852a07-1e77-54f8-9037-1f17a12c391f" :question/math-academy-id "q-221767" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y.$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $3$\n\nI. $[0]$\n\nII. $[1]$\n\nIII. $[2]$" :question/worked-solution "We have $3\\in [x]$ if $3∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\cdot 3 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\pi  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x \\\\ -1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $3\\notin [0]$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)=1\\ne -1\\,\\times $\n\n- $3\\in [1]$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)=-1\\,✓$\n\n- $3\\notin [2]$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 2)=1\\ne -1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dacfbca6-6135-5d51-a07e-627fce79dfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0870007d-28f6-5394-a3f7-191763ad0726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421634} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8f1e44-03e2-51d0-9aa1-2173d810665d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421644} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa42817-8906-5d24-8213-162156ed884a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421645} {:answer/id #uuid "5812dc9d-ae67-5387-a7a7-136b1f26b10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421658} {:answer/id #uuid "8f50ab69-dd3e-5316-98aa-d4140b7a3a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f50ab69-dd3e-5316-98aa-d4140b7a3a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421671} :answer-field/id #uuid "648ceec9-0c7f-519e-9b1b-ce3a5608f2be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d844129-f571-5c80-934e-cfc1faad53cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-221722" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $11\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $-6?$\n\nI. $[5]$\n\nII. $[-17]$\n\nIII. $[-8]$" :question/worked-solution "We have $-6\\in [x]$ if $-6∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11\\,|\\,(-6-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $-6\\in [5]$ because $11\\,|\\,(-6-5)\\,✓$\n\n- $-6\\in [-17]$ because $11\\,|\\,(-6+17)\\,✓$\n\n- $-6\\notin [-8]$ because $11∤(-6+8)\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3601ac83-0800-567a-9a0d-4d5c37cef413" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to an Equivalence Class"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fb079ab-f666-5cc0-acd0-dc79af768608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421652} {:answer/id #uuid "641a1fbc-83f5-514f-b0d5-77a3f28046d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421661} {:answer/id #uuid "beda1346-455c-59a5-b6e2-690d2703d950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421684} {:answer/id #uuid "d8687927-2b83-51b2-aa18-5333115cbc1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421693} {:answer/id #uuid "e261b60f-981d-5dea-add0-42767006246e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e261b60f-981d-5dea-add0-42767006246e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421697} :answer-field/id #uuid "11f848d9-afc8-5368-b1fd-07597c4b200a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9e1ee25-17a0-56ec-9f69-bcad0ae7d0cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-221717" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[0]?$\n\nI. $2\\pi $\n\nII. $3\\pi $\n\nIII. $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[0]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $0$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1} \\\\  & ={…,-4\\pi ,-2\\pi ,0,2\\pi ,4\\pi ,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $2\\pi $ is in $[0],$ while $3\\pi $ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3601ac83-0800-567a-9a0d-4d5c37cef413" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to an Equivalence Class"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23beadc4-1a1f-515e-995f-8df492e565df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421641} {:answer/id #uuid "3927cc39-bdb6-50da-bb44-c5b049718f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421648} {:answer/id #uuid "6436b354-fb27-5476-8641-51d7906d4663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421662} {:answer/id #uuid "81eda3bf-1013-5fe7-84cd-f856f077edc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421668} {:answer/id #uuid "fc29ca23-eb46-59b2-b550-672eac436718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc29ca23-eb46-59b2-b550-672eac436718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421701} :answer-field/id #uuid "4af3f260-723a-5617-9400-73aac1989855" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb353c96-604c-5c65-9adf-47096b9ec124" :question/math-academy-id "q-86223" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt[3]{y}.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[-8]?$\n\nI. $-8$\n\nII. $8$\n\nIII. $2$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[-8]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $-8$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-8] & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼-8} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt[3]{-8}} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}=-2} \\\\  & ={-8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-8$ is in $[-8],$ while $8$ and $2$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dacfbca6-6135-5d51-a07e-627fce79dfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52c8fe5b-d375-581b-9b3a-52597f4673c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421655} {:answer/id #uuid "712da55b-bdaa-506a-bc71-7d0285b9be2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421665} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8e86ce-b4e2-5ece-9883-422f68961751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421690} {:answer/id #uuid "dd6864e6-1d99-5d8e-b6f4-0cdb7769034b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421696} {:answer/id #uuid "ef949561-2cb0-5e30-8ccf-97ae2720042d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd8e86ce-b4e2-5ece-9883-422f68961751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421690} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2fd66d6-e133-56c3-81e9-9ab5d15cb8ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c88f13fc-8d9b-5fc7-ac75-4d5797158af8" :question/math-academy-id "q-86231" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $2\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $9?$\n\nI. $[5]$\n\nII. $[2]$\n\nIII. $[19]$" :question/worked-solution "We have $9\\in [x]$ if $9∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,|\\,(9-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $9\\in [5]$ because $2\\,|\\,(9-5)\\,✓$\n\n- $9\\notin [2]$ because $2∤(9-2)\\,\\times $\n\n- $9\\in [19]$ because $2\\,|\\,(9-19)\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dacfbca6-6135-5d51-a07e-627fce79dfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Classes Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bd4c6cf-cad2-50eb-8893-138f4922e512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421650} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa231d2-67a6-5c53-87ae-7c44aba80acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421651} {:answer/id #uuid "561c2307-984b-58e7-aebc-35715b1b1d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421657} {:answer/id #uuid "5a8128d1-2683-5c3d-9347-079db2426780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421659} {:answer/id #uuid "bc38deb0-31a4-57b8-936c-ff1eb6dda150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "561c2307-984b-58e7-aebc-35715b1b1d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421657} :answer-field/id #uuid "5267f8e2-d7a6-55fe-b248-6c38530bb2a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd41a54f-8627-52be-9ff1-ad5548f86090" :question/math-academy-id "q-77214" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $3\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $5?$\n\nI. $[3]$\n\nII. $[-1]$\n\nIII. $[8]$" :question/worked-solution "We have $5\\in [x]$ if $5∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,|\\,(5-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $5\\notin [3]$ because $3∤(5-3)\\,\\times $\n\n- $5\\in [-1]$ because $3\\,|\\,(5-(-1))\\,✓$\n\n- $5\\in [8]$ because $3\\,|\\,(5-8)\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2573a21a-8e89-50df-979f-28afe2a36547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421792} {:answer/id #uuid "8c15b390-e3ab-578b-9938-8a4a3d2c7655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421801} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc3278d-03ee-5ec6-8887-70c78218b07b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421805} {:answer/id #uuid "9d075126-4b78-5a5b-8783-75c3171a8fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421807} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d3143b-2b4d-55bb-b86b-df87737b53d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c15b390-e3ab-578b-9938-8a4a3d2c7655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421801} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5729148-37a9-5323-892c-ea554a3ec7c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70cf3885-2e2c-5971-b49c-1988c3e0bb2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130004" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-2y=0,$ where $x>0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nIII. The general solution of the equation is $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}+B{x}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}})+2x(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})-2(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(12{x}^{2})+2x(4{x}^{3})-2{x}^{4}=18{x}^{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is false.\n\n- So, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution but ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution of the equation, hence $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}+B{x}^{4}$ is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0246f7ce-f1b2-5e93-8e63-e7393dc569b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421786} {:answer/id #uuid "70e8c5bf-b371-5452-9286-8ac280de0cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421797} {:answer/id #uuid "78ece1af-ad7d-5ae8-b110-54b1d28cb817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421798} {:answer/id #uuid "96de1655-9970-5892-87e1-0ac2221ac207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421803} {:answer/id #uuid "fda802d2-36b2-5ed7-834d-f564fbf27833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fda802d2-36b2-5ed7-834d-f564fbf27833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421813} :answer-field/id #uuid "9035e657-b711-5639-a608-83ef4fb511b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fd65812-f6ae-584d-bfe8-d11797aed930" :question/math-academy-id "q-130000" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}-4y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=x{e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nIII. The general solution of the equation is $y=Ax{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =(1+x){e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =(2+x){e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n(2+x){e}^{x}-4(x{e}^{x})=(2-3x){e}^{x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is false.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n4{e}^{2x}-4{e}^{2x}=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution while ${y}_{2}$ is a solution of the equation, hence $y=Ax{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$ is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45672c57-a19d-5a8b-ac6b-6ea21293b5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423464} {:answer/id #uuid "97210339-8c0b-5905-a2df-f3b57f435e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423475} {:answer/id #uuid "c03a15a1-3e13-53ce-9c92-762f8bc2417d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423483} {:answer/id #uuid "cc45f2dd-0a53-53f5-b140-3b2a83fa50cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423485} {:answer/id #uuid "d1969766-6b47-59dc-a429-472653bbb6d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45672c57-a19d-5a8b-ac6b-6ea21293b5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423464} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7528fa6-219c-52b3-82fe-4826326f572d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa0b27c3-d4dd-5c0c-8989-66af8729df06" :question/math-academy-id "q-161029" :question/problem "![](images/12/1252844f7a9e2fb2ca0ff1d2e2e1546a460e7d0846b4ae2996b6ce984ee305a1.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/49/49aa7d6ccd4b14b48ede7486f41ab11ac22b593c06d75795fe7819889b2b2f23.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\n- When $u=\\frac{1}{4},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46101349-6d6a-5bb0-9207-0aaec4554d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424337} {:answer/id #uuid "49dca3bb-2186-5f51-be14-255602c25375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424338} {:answer/id #uuid "f32cc730-85a1-5c7e-be94-acff281af5b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424362} {:answer/id #uuid "f4459918-d225-5b1f-98b0-e5d6e8d3db04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424363} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b18ca0-cc56-5a67-b890-09a9290c08a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f32cc730-85a1-5c7e-be94-acff281af5b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424362} :answer-field/id #uuid "0328f54f-4ff1-538a-9504-a91aff1cf880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "239e34ce-3c73-5bae-b143-3d41bff8bf51" :question/math-academy-id "q-97754" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The temperature of a town on a certain day.\n\nII. The number of letters of a word randomly selected from an English dictionary.\n\nIII. The number of typos in a randomly selected book." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because a temperature is a real number, so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely (there are more short words than very long words, for example).\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of typos can be any positive integer. Moreover, each outcome is not equally likely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e3e30ef-68e6-519f-85f4-fee8d10f81e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424336} {:answer/id #uuid "61ef3bcd-05c5-505d-b1b7-15232aa92580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424341} {:answer/id #uuid "6d59c617-fb6d-55c2-8065-4b569c87d39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424345} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e4752b-5097-5dca-a4d5-bd8632d6c5c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424353} {:answer/id #uuid "c4370a56-f375-577d-973d-adc95a739c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d59c617-fb6d-55c2-8065-4b569c87d39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424345} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e981e36-68c1-59b6-8484-c1aaf524b056" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78d51529-5603-5294-836c-698a99289cb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-148394" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when picking an integer between $0$ and $10$ at random.\n\nII. The number obtained when picking a real number between $5$ and $9$ at random.\n\nIII. The number of clients arriving at a shopping mall in a day." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of integers between $0$ and $10,$ and since the selection is random, each integer has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are infinitely many real numbers between $5$ and $9,$ and so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of clients arriving at a shopping mall can be any positive integer, and each outcome is not equally likely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e218fff-12f9-5fa5-922d-87fcb0d3466e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424332} {:answer/id #uuid "59a9fa70-556c-52b8-b78c-ec478f8ef8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424340} {:answer/id #uuid "8b023963-20ab-5f85-8fbc-5c210e36192b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424349} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed4e290-9d27-5fac-8fe0-eb7027b7fb73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424350} {:answer/id #uuid "b51a8979-70c9-5fd8-88fb-2353345ae803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e218fff-12f9-5fa5-922d-87fcb0d3466e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424332} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d297b53-634d-5917-86bf-fd5c5bf44384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc3edf0b-9469-5401-acc3-594a44dac66e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97753" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when picking a card from a set of cards numbered from $1$ to $12,$ at random.\n\nII. The number obtained when throwing a standard die that is biased toward landing on $1.$\n\nIII. The color obtained when a ball from a bag containing $5$ balls with distinct colors is chosen randomly." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of cards, and since the selection is made at random, each card has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Since the die is not fair, each outcome is not equally likely, because there is a higher probability of getting $1.$\n\n- Random variable III can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of distinct colors, and since the selection is made at random, each color has an equal probability of being picked.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c76b2b1b-7536-5b3c-a4c4-6f5e5b72e6ec" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Number by Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05897e61-41a9-514e-b9be-e42a73520d18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424633} {:answer/id #uuid "2a734bd6-c5da-5171-a2e1-5a7554594aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424656} {:answer/id #uuid "636c0f10-fd0f-5aa1-b437-63bb6b77b56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424687} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cb110f-2081-5793-9d42-322cb1d7ddb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424761} {:answer/id #uuid "ea83ba1b-49b7-5c2b-ae70-9177785e563b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "636c0f10-fd0f-5aa1-b437-63bb6b77b56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424687} :answer-field/id #uuid "da79e43d-9229-50b1-8f6e-db23fb52b3ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3913baef-cd96-5f27-bb8b-fbabac946e82" :question/math-academy-id "q-43156" :question/problem "Which of the following is undefined?\n\nI. $0\\cdot 0$\n\nII. $\\frac{2}{0}$\n\nIII. $\\frac{0}{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "We consider each point individually:\n\n- Multiplying any number by zero always gives zero. Therefore, $0\\cdot 0=0.$\n\n- We cannot divide any number by zero. Therefore, $\\frac{2}{0}=2÷0$ is undefined.\n\n- Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore, $\\frac{0}{-1}=0÷(-1)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a25bce44-98ca-54c6-a59f-b7508880d5ba" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Number is Divisible by 4"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "482c5dae-4d41-560e-9cce-646d022e2981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424670} {:answer/id #uuid "819e1052-6513-50b0-bd5c-5e10197a5eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424708} {:answer/id #uuid "83bf1713-3353-59a3-b698-75b1396a3acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424711} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e28211-0c01-504c-9700-eb6226c25b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424730} {:answer/id #uuid "d8585b9e-3cc0-56bc-af72-b6f310e2d8f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8585b9e-3cc0-56bc-af72-b6f310e2d8f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424750} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c05dc84-ff51-5384-9b95-c62170ff697c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef2c41de-4b26-57f5-bd27-cd0dbe211bfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-131860" :question/problem "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $123\\,321\\,87◻$ divisible by $4?$\n\nI. $2$\n\nII. $4$\n\nIII. $8$" :question/worked-solution "A number is divisible by $4$ if the last two digits form a number that is divisible by $4.$\n\nThe last two digits of the number $123\\,321\\,87◻$ are $7◻.$\n\n- If $2$ is substituted into the blank, then the last two digits are $72,$ which is indeed divisible by $4.$\n\n- If $4$ is substituted into the blank, then the last two digits are $74,$ which is *not* divisible by $4.$\n\n- If $8$ is substituted into the blank, then the last two digits are $78,$ which is *not* divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef650886-8d46-583e-bad0-1429ba496479" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "289c50a0-ecfe-528e-8457-52cef5a9eea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424911} {:answer/id #uuid "7272d0c8-fe13-518b-af81-9a1e813b6be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424932} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1f5ec0-d9fa-5898-9b3f-1ebb2130be4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424937} {:answer/id #uuid "955ea776-bc16-5188-8524-157fb43af2d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text}